// Package match ranks library titles against what Alexa heard and decides whether to play the best one, ask which one was meant, or report nothing found.
//
// An Index holds one library in one language and runs this pipeline per Search:
//
//  1. Normalise. Normalize folds case and diacritics, drops punctuation and leading articles, joins runs of single letters ("E.T." -> "et"), turns roman numerals after a word, number words, ordinals and spoken years into digits ("rocky second" -> "rocky 2", "nineteen eighty four" -> "1984") and drops "part"/"chapter" before a number. Each supported language (en, de, fr, es, it) has its own articles, number words and conjunctions; others use English rules. Titles and queries go through the same function, so both sides meet in the middle.
//  2. Alias. At Upsert every Doc expands to aliases: its title and Doc.Aliases, a variant with single-letter roman numerals converted ("Rocky V" -> "rocky 5"), German umlauts spelt out ("ü" -> "ue"), and derived shortenings that drop a subtitle, keep only the subtitle or drop a parenthetical ("Dune: Part Two" -> "Dune", "Avengers: Endgame" -> "Endgame"). Derived aliases score with a penalty and never count as exact.
//  3. Retrieve. An in-memory inverted index finds a candidate pool of about 200 Docs with a disjunction of fuzzy word matches (within one or two edits of a word, keeping its first letter), phonetic key matches (Double Metaphone, or Cologne phonetics for German) and, when those find little, character trigrams, ranked by TF-IDF. Docs whose primary alias equals the query are always added. Retrieval only needs recall; its scores are never exposed. bleve, or a simpler trigram retriever for small libraries, can stand in through Config.Retriever.
//  4. Score. Each candidate is scored against each alias and keeps its best: s = max(token_set_ratio, 0.9 * partial_ratio when the query is shorter, ratio without spaces, 0.9 when the strings sound identical), then 0.45*s + 0.25*Jaro-Winkler + 0.15*OSA similarity + 0.15*phonetic overlap, scaled down for query words the alias does not account for (numbers must match exactly). An exact match of a primary alias scores 1 and fuzzy matches are capped below it. A trailing release year in the query ("dune 2021") is matched against Doc.Year. Doc.Boost and Query.Hints add at most Config.MaxBoost.
//  5. Decide. With c the top score and m its lead over the runner-up: play when exactly one Doc matched exactly, when c >= 0.90 (with a lead of at least 0.02), or when c >= 0.80 and m >= 0.10; ask when several Docs matched exactly or c >= 0.60; otherwise not found. Thresholds live in Config.
package match
